WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:13.158
December 17 the real bank robber was arrested.

2
00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:19.842
On that day London opinion changed for the second time since the wager had begun—this time in Mr.

3
00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:20.542
Fogg's favor.

4
00:00:21.503 --> 00:00:25.925
The papers, which had presented him as a criminal, reported him an honorable gentleman again.

5
00:00:26.866 --> 00:00:30.708
They resumed their discussion about the wager, and once again, bets were placed.

6
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.224
December 17 was the seventy-sixth day of the eighty-day race.

7
00:00:37.105 --> 00:00:41.308
Mr. Fogg's five friends at the Reform Club passed the remaining days in suspense.

8
00:00:42.069 --> 00:00:44.671
During the time when everyone thought he was a thief, Mr.

9
00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:46.813
Fogg's friends had not expected him to return.

10
00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.796
Now they realized the wager was still on.

11
00:00:50.737 --> 00:00:51.317
Where was Mr.

12
00:00:51.337 --> 00:00:53.820
Fogg? No news had been received from him.

13
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:55.241
Was he dead?

14
00:00:55.861 --> 00:00:56.522
Had he given up?

15
00:00:57.183 --> 00:00:58.544
Was he continuing his journey?

16
00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:04.607
A huge crowd collected outside the Reform Club on the evening of Saturday, December 21.

17
00:01:05.448 --> 00:01:10.371
Traffic was interrupted, and arguments, discussions, and last-minute bets excited the crowd.

18
00:01:11.452 --> 00:01:16.075
As the hour approached when Phileas Fogg was due, the excitement rose to its highest pitch.

19
00:01:17.056 --> 00:01:19.657
The five friends waited in the great saloon of the club.

20
00:01:20.358 --> 00:01:26.082
When the clock indicated twenty minutes past eight, one member said, "What time did the last train arrive

21
00:01:26.102 --> 00:01:26.702
from Liverpool?"

22
00:01:26.722 --> 00:01:30.428
"At twenty-three minutes past seven," another answered.

23
00:01:31.449 --> 00:01:37.612
"Well, gentlemen, if Phileas Fogg had come on the 7:23 train, he would have gotten here by now.

24
00:01:38.532 --> 00:01:41.693
We can, therefore, regard the bet as won."

25
00:01:41.713 --> 00:01:45.075
"Wait. Let us not be too hasty.

26
00:01:46.095 --> 00:01:47.736
Mr. Fogg is an eccentric man.

27
00:01:48.636 --> 00:01:50.077
His punctuality is well known.

28
00:01:50.697 --> 00:01:52.878
He never arrives too soon or too late.

29
00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:56.480
I will not be surprised if he appears here at the last minute."

30
00:01:57.444 --> 00:02:00.486
Soon the hands on the clock pointed to twenty minutes to nine.

31
00:02:00.506 --> 00:02:03.668
"Five more minutes," someone noted.

32
00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:06.290
The five gentlemen looked at each other.

33
00:02:07.131 --> 00:02:08.932
Their anxiety was becoming intense.

34
00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:12.034
The clock advanced to eighteen minutes to nine.

35
00:02:12.815 --> 00:02:14.356
Then to seventeen minutes to nine.

36
00:02:15.136 --> 00:02:16.237
They all fell silent.

37
00:02:16.998 --> 00:02:19.179
Only the murmurs of the crowd outside could be heard.

38
00:02:19.199 --> 00:02:23.242
"Sixteen minutes to nine," one of the gentlemen noted.

39
00:02:24.122 --> 00:02:27.056
One more minute and the wager would be won.

40
00:02:27.076 --> 00:02:32.199
At the fifty-fifth second, a loud cry was heard from the street, followed by applause and cheers.

41
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.621
The five men rose from their seats.

42
00:02:35.581 --> 00:02:38.623
At the fifty-seventh second, the door of the saloon opened.

43
00:02:39.543 --> 00:02:44.466
Phileas Fogg stepped in, followed by an excited crowd that forced its way through the club doors.

44
00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:47.368
In his usual calm voice, Mr.

45
00:02:47.408 --> 00:02:49.349
Fogg said, "Here I am, gentlemen."

46
00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.871
Yes, Phileas Fogg had won the bet.

47
00:02:53.432 --> 00:02:53.852
But how?

48
00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:58.814
Well, as instructed, Passepartout had hurried to Reverend Wilson's house.

49
00:02:59.675 --> 00:03:04.457
He was not at home, and Passepartout waited twenty minutes before the reverend strolled up to the door.

50
00:03:05.558 --> 00:03:10.120
They exchanged only a few words when Passepartout turned and ran as fast as he could!

51
00:03:12.342 --> 00:03:17.845
He leaped over vendors' carts, knocked over pedestrians, and in three minutes, was at Saville Row again.

52
00:03:17.865 --> 00:03:19.866
"What is the matter?"

53
00:03:20.226 --> 00:03:20.766
asked Mr.

54
00:03:20.786 --> 00:03:26.793
Fogg. "My master marriage impossible Passepartout gasped.

55
00:03:27.853 --> 00:03:29.494
"Impossible?" asked Fogg.

56
00:03:29.514 --> 00:03:32.074
"Impossible for tomorrow."

57
00:03:32.094 --> 00:03:35.595
"Why?" "Because tomorrow is not Monday!"

58
00:03:35.936 --> 00:03:36.856
cried Passepartout.

59
00:03:36.876 --> 00:03:39.777
"Tomorrow is Sunday, and today is Saturday!

60
00:03:40.517 --> 00:03:41.617
We have made a mistake.

61
00:03:42.498 --> 00:03:43.918
There are only ten minutes left!

62
00:03:44.698 --> 00:03:45.478
You must go!"

63
00:03:46.839 --> 00:03:50.300
Passepartout then grabbed his master's collar and dragged him out of the house.

64
00:03:51.604 --> 00:03:55.066
Mr. Fogg jumped into a cab and promised the cabman a hundred pounds.

65
00:03:56.087 --> 00:04:02.070
Ignoring the whistles of five traffic police and causing three accidents, the cabman delivered Fogg and his friends

66
00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:02.950
at the Reform Club.

67
00:04:03.951 --> 00:04:08.554
At a quarter before nine, Phileas Fogg had walked into the great saloon and won his wager!

68
00:04:09.454 --> 00:04:10.955
But where had he miscalculated?

69
00:04:11.695 --> 00:04:13.176
The cause of the error was simple.

70
00:04:13.876 --> 00:04:16.578
Phileas Fogg had been traveling constantly eastward.

71
00:04:17.438 --> 00:04:19.780
In journeying eastward he had gone toward the sun.

72
00:04:20.812 --> 00:04:25.475
Therefore, each day was shortened by four minutes for every degree of longitude he crossed.

73
00:04:26.815 --> 00:04:30.077
The circumference of the earth is measured in 360 degrees.

74
00:04:30.858 --> 00:04:35.300
Three hundred and sixty degrees multiplied by four is exactly 24 hours.

75
00:04:36.061 --> 00:04:38.502
Without realizing it Fogg had gained a day.

76
00:04:39.503 --> 00:04:40.543
That evening Mr.

77
00:04:40.563 --> 00:04:44.986
Fogg, as tranquil as ever, said to Aouda, "Is our marriage still agreeable to you?"

78
00:04:45.006 --> 00:04:48.668
"It is for me to ask that question," she said.

79
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.882
"You were ruined, but now you are rich again."

80
00:04:51.902 --> 00:04:54.525
"Pardon me, madam," said Fogg.

81
00:04:54.545 --> 00:04:56.547
"But my fortune belongs to you.

82
00:04:57.548 --> 00:05:03.513
If you had not suggested our marriage, Passepartout would not have gone to the reverend's house—" "Dear Mr.

83
00:05:03.573 --> 00:05:04.894
Fogg!" she stopped him.

84
00:05:04.914 --> 00:05:06.376
"Dear Aouda!"

85
00:05:06.676 --> 00:05:08.077
he replied with his whole heart.

86
00:05:08.898 --> 00:05:11.661
Mr. Fogg and Aouda were married 48 hours later.

87
00:05:12.401 --> 00:05:15.044
Passepartout, glowing with pride, gave the bride away.

88
00:05:18.376 --> 00:05:21.138
Mr. Fogg had traveled around the world in eighty days.

89
00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:26.662
He had employed every means of vehicle, from trains and steamers to elephants and sledges.

90
00:05:27.523 --> 00:05:29.905
In the end he was no richer than when he set out.

91
00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:31.286
No richer?

92
00:05:31.866 --> 00:05:35.569
Perhaps, but he had gained a love that made him the happiest of men!

93
00:05:36.429 --> 00:05:39.011
Would you not travel around the world for a chance at the same?

94
00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:45.076
Mr. Fogg won his wager!

95
00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:49.735
Thank goodness for those funny lines on maps—longitude and latitude.

96
00:05:50.575 --> 00:05:53.477
On a map, time zones are marked by lines of longitude.

97
00:05:54.518 --> 00:05:57.100
The lines divide the globe into sections, or time zones.

98
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.423
Each section is one hour ahead of the section to its west and one hour behind the one

99
00:06:02.463 --> 00:06:03.064
to its east.

100
00:06:04.525 --> 00:06:05.626
Look closely at the map.

101
00:06:06.346 --> 00:06:08.928
Can you see the lines that run across the globe east to west?

102
00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:12.310
They are called lines of latitude.

103
00:06:13.031 --> 00:06:14.852
Do you know the most famous line of latitude?

104
00:06:15.712 --> 00:06:16.553
I'll give you a clue.

105
00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:18.475
It's very hot there.

106
00:06:19.236 --> 00:06:20.858
That's right–the equator!

107
00:06:21.619 --> 00:06:23.942
The equator runs right around the middle of the earth.

108
00:06:25.323 --> 00:06:27.466
Well, I must say good-bye to you now.

109
00:06:28.407 --> 00:06:30.109
I hope you've enjoyed traveling with Mr.

110
00:06:30.149 --> 00:06:32.692
Fogg and me around the world—it's been quite an adventure, hasn't it?
